# System architecture: a recipe, to copy and adapt (pikslide --help
# architecture).
#
# A system architecture diagram shows the parts of a system and what each
# one talks to. This one draws a Django web system: Nginx in front, Django
# under Gunicorn, PostgreSQL, Redis as the cache and task queue, and a
# Celery worker, the server side running as docker compose services.
#
# THE SYMBOLS, AND WHAT DRAWS EACH
# a service or program -- box "Django" bold "Gunicorn (WSGI)" small
# a database or queue -- cylinder "PostgreSQL" bold "database" small
# files -- file "Static files" bold "static/, media/" small
# a call, from caller to callee -- arrow "SQL" above
# a boundary, such as a host, a network or a deployment -- a box behind
#   the parts: fill bg1 darker 5% stroke dashed
#
# HOW IT'S LAID OUT
# - Every part is one size: boxwid, cylwid, filewid and their heights are
#   set once, at the top, so that the parts line up on a grid. Each is its
#   name in bold over its role in small text, short enough to fit.
# - Arrows point from caller to callee, labeled with what passes along
#   them: a protocol (HTTPS, SQL) or what it's for (cache, tasks). Those
#   inside the boundary are all 0.8in; the browser's is longer, to keep its
#   label clear of the boundary's edge.
# - The second row sits under the first: each part in it at (X.x, row2),
#   under the part that uses it, so that the arrows between the rows are
#   straight, and Redis and Celery share a height, so the arrow between
#   them is too.
# - The boundary is drawn last, sized from the parts' edges, with room at
#   the top for its title, its center worked out into variables first
#   (midx, midy). `behind Browser`, the very first object, puts it at the
#   back: behind Nginx, it would hide the end of the HTTPS arrow, drawn
#   before Nginx. Its fill is a theme color, so that it follows a
#   --template.
# - Outside services, such as a mail server or object storage, go outside
#   the boundary, an arrow crossing it. Several hosts or networks are a
#   boundary each, side by side or nested, each sized from what's in it.

boxwid = 1.4in
boxht = 0.65in
cylwid = 1.4in
cylht = 0.8in
filewid = 1.4in
fileht = 0.8in

Browser: box "Browser"
arrow "HTTPS" above right 1.2in
Nginx: box "Nginx" bold "reverse proxy" small
arrow "HTTP" above right 0.8in
Django: box "Django" bold "Gunicorn (WSGI)" small
arrow "SQL" above right 0.8in
DB: cylinder "PostgreSQL" bold "database" small

row2 = Django.y - 1.5in
Static: file "Static files" bold "static/, media/" small at (Nginx.x, row2)
Redis: cylinder "Redis" bold "cache, task queue" small at (Django.x, row2)
Worker: box "Celery" bold "background tasks" small at (DB.x, row2)

arrow "files" ljust from Nginx.s to Static.n
arrow "cache, tasks" ljust from Django.s to Redis.n
arrow "tasks" above from Worker.w to Redis.e
arrow "SQL" ljust from Worker.n to DB.s

# The services' frame, drawn behind everything: wide enough for both
# rows, with room for its title at the top.
midx = (Nginx.w.x + DB.e.x) / 2
midy = (DB.n.y + Redis.s.y) / 2
Compose: box wid (DB.e.x - Nginx.w.x) + 0.5in ht (DB.n.y - Redis.s.y) + 0.8in \
    at (midx, midy + 0.15in) fill bg1 darker 5% stroke dashed behind Browser
"docker compose" bold at 0.25in below Compose.n
